---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro'
import JBrowseFigure from '../components/JBrowseFigure.astro'
import { catalog } from '../../../packages/examples/src/examples/catalog'
import { specs } from '../../../scripts/screenshots/specs.mjs'
import { f12CombinedCloseupUrl } from '../lib/f12CombinedLinks'
import {
  brafV600,
  buildOnOpen,
  proteinLinked,
  tp53PhmmerRp15,
  tp53Protein3d,
  tp53R248,
  tp53UnirefBrowser,
} from '../lib/jbrowseLinks'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL

// The figures and captions come from the examples catalog, which the examples
// page also reads. Each figure names a screenshot spec
// (scripts/screenshots/specs.mjs), and that spec's `?data=` URL is the live link
// under the image, so the figure and the link open the same state. A compose
// spec stacks other captures and has no state of its own, so it gets no link.
//
// The links point at the deployed app instead of `${base}/demo/`, as the user
// guide's live links do, because the deploy's link checker does not understand a
// query string on an internal link.
const figures = catalog.flatMap(entry =>
  (entry.figures ?? []).map(figure => {
    const spec = specs.find(s => s.name === figure.src)
    if (!spec) {
      throw new Error(`gallery figure ${figure.src} has no screenshot spec`)
    }
    return {
      ...figure,
      example: entry,
      href:
        spec.url === undefined
          ? undefined
          : `https://gmod.org/JBrowseMSA/demo/${spec.url}`,
    }
  }),
)
---

<Base
  title="Gallery — JBrowseMSA"
  description="A gallery of react-msaview: alignments that each carry a biological finding, and connected JBrowse sessions linking genome, alignment, and 3D structure."
  wide
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Gallery</h1>
    <p>
      Two kinds of example: the standalone react-msaview viewer showing an
      alignment and tree, and react-msaview running inside JBrowse, connected to
      a genome view and 3D structure. Scripts in the repo build each one from
      public data.
    </p>

    <h2 id="standalone">Standalone react-msaview</h2>
    <p>
      These figures show an alignment, a tree and optional feature overlays,
      with no genome browser. Each figure shows one viewer capability: domain
      overlays, reference diffing, data layers, tree topology, RNA secondary
      structure, base-pair arcs, gene arrows, base-resolution DNA, scale. Click
      one to open the demo app in the state it was captured in; the figure and
      the link come from the same screenshot spec. The
      <a href={`${base}/examples`}>examples</a> page has the full set, live and
      with their source.
    </p>

    {figures.map(f => (
      <Figure src={`${f.src}.png`} alt={f.caption} href={f.href}>
        <strong>{f.title}.</strong> {f.caption}
      </Figure>
    ))}

    <h2 id="jbrowse">Inside JBrowse</h2>
    <p>
      react-msaview also runs as a
      <a href="https://github.com/GMOD/jbrowse-plugin-msaview">
        JBrowse 2 plugin
      </a>
      : the alignment and tree open <em>inside</em> JBrowse, connected to a
      genome view and, with
      <a href="https://github.com/GMOD/jbrowse-plugin-protein3d">protein3d</a>,
      the AlphaFold structure. Selecting a region in any view highlights the
      matching columns, codons, and residues in the others. Each example below
      is one declarative URL.
    </p>

    <JBrowseFigure
      src="f12-combined-closeup.png"
      href={f12CombinedCloseupUrl}
      alt="A single JBrowse session at base resolution over F12 exon 3: the RefSeq gene annotation track on top and the react-msaview DNA alignment with its species tree below. The four cetaceans show a gap where every other mammal has a C, the shared single-base frameshift, and the highlighted column links the genome to the alignment"
      body="Genome ↔ alignment in one JBrowse session: the F12 locus (RefSeq annotation, top) over the react-msaview DNA alignment + species tree, zoomed to base resolution at exon 3. The highlighted column links the genome to the alignment. The four cetaceans carry a single-base deletion (a gap where every other mammal has a C) that disables the gene. The 14-exon model is registered to the alignment columns and lifted onto each species."
    />

    <JBrowseFigure
      src="genome-browser-tp53-protein3d.png"
      href={tp53Protein3d}
      alt="JBrowse: the TP53 gene with ClinVar, the p53 ortholog alignment, and the AlphaFold structure, all connected, with the nuclear export signal motif highlighted across genome, alignment, and 3D structure"
      body="Genome ↔ alignment ↔ 3D structure: the TP53 gene (RefSeq + ClinVar), the p53 ortholog alignment, and the AlphaFold structure as one connected session. It opens with the nuclear export signal motif (residues 339–350) highlighted on the genome, the alignment columns, and the folded structure."
    />

    <p>The same connected pattern without the structure view:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href={proteinLinked} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          SRC ↗
        </a>{' '}
        — the Src-family kinase alignment beside the SRC gene.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={brafV600} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          BRAF V600E ↗
        </a>{' '}
        — the RAF family on the invariant V600 codon, with a ClinVar
        pathogenic-variant track.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={tp53R248} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          TP53 R248 ↗
        </a>{' '}
        — p53 across vertebrates on the R248 hotspot, with ClinVar.
      </li>
    </ul>

    {buildOnOpen.live && (
      <>
        <p>
          The links above carry their alignments as files. The two below carry a
          request instead, and the view builds the alignment when it opens: the
          spec names the transcript in <code>connectedTranscript</code> and the
          plugin looks its exon model up in the genome view's gene track, so the
          whole session is a URL short enough to type.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href={tp53UnirefBrowser} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              TP53 UniRef cluster ↗
            </a>{' '}
            — every reference-proteome protein in UniProtKB within 50% identity
            of p53, looked up from UniProt and aligned to the transcript in the
            browser. It submits no job to any service, so it opens in seconds.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={tp53PhmmerRp15} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              TP53 phmmer search ↗
            </a>{' '}
            — the same gene through a phmmer search of UniProt's representative
            proteomes at 15% similarity, the widest net EBI offers: p53's
            relatives across the tree of life. The wait is EBI's queue, from
            seconds to many minutes.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </>
    )}

    <h2 id="embedded">Embedding one panel</h2>
    <p>
      A connected session shows three linked views, each with its own tracks,
      menus and coordinate system. A page with one question can embed the viewer
      alone, with only the controls that question needs around it: the alignment
      without the genome browser, the tree turned off when there is no
      meaningful tree, a diff toggle and an expand button beside it.
    </p>
    {/* No `#controls-from-props` fragment here: ExampleBrowser reads the hash
      as a route instead of scrolling to an element, so the examples page is
      client:only and carries no such id. hyperlink --check-anchors reports that
      as a broken anchor and fails the build. */}
    <p>
      The embedded panel is a react-msaview component in your own page, and its
      props update it live, so a toggle costs no remount and no refetch. The
      <a href={`${base}/embedding`}>embedding guide</a> has the recipe, and the
      <strong>Controls from props</strong> entry on the
      <a href={`${base}/examples`}>examples</a> page runs it.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
